1	//! The corpus recipe — the declarative half of `xerj corpus build`.
2	//!
3	//! A recipe says WHERE records come from, which of their fields carry
4	//! IDENTITY (so the same logical record arriving from three sources becomes
5	//! one), how fields merge across sources, and which derived fields to
6	//! compute. It is TOML on purpose: it is human-authored, commented — licence
7	//! reasoning belongs next to the source it justifies — and it ships verbatim
8	//! inside the built pack as provenance.
9	//!
10	//! The tool is deliberately schema-agnostic: the recipe maps whatever the
11	//! sources actually contain onto a small query envelope (`title`, `body`,
12	//! `defs`, …). Nothing here knows what a vulnerability is. The parse is
13	//! STRICT — unknown keys and bad values are loud errors, not silently
14	//! ignored defaults — because a typo'd `aliases` silently turns one unique
15	//! record into three, which is the exact failure this subsystem exists to
16	//! prevent. (Hub manifests are tolerant because they are untrusted input
17	//! validated elsewhere; a recipe is trusted author input and must round-trip
18	//! faithfully.)
19	
