1	//! Input-format adapters: one function per known record FORMAT, each turning
2	//! a source value into flat key paths.
3	//!
4	//! An adapter knows the INPUT format, never our domain: `osv` understands
5	//! the ossf/osv-schema shape (shared by osv.dev dumps and github-reviewed
6	//! GHSA files); `rustsec-md` understands RustSec's native advisory file
7	//! (TOML frontmatter + markdown — the mechanism is generic frontmatter
8	//! extraction, the curated table names are upstream's); `flat` does generic
9	//! flattening for everything else. Which keys of the result feed the query
10	//! envelope is the recipe's job, not the adapter's — the same `osv` adapter
11	//! would serve a Go vulnerability corpus unchanged.
12	//!
13	//! Flattening mirrors `extract::flatten_object` semantics (two levels of
14	//! nesting become `a_b` keys; deeper structure and arrays-of-objects are
15	//! stored as JSON strings; arrays of scalars stay arrays) so a record
16	//! normalized here does not transform a second time at index time.
17	//!
18	//! One deliberate omission: OSV `database_specific` is NEVER read. The
19	//! schema marks it "intended for internal use only" and "subject to change
20	//! without notice" — building on it means building on a field its owner
21	//! says may vanish. Ecosystem-specific fields ARE read (they are the
22	//! documented extension point upstreams actually publish to, and RustSec's
23	//! affected-functions list lives there).
